diff --git a/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..fe0fea6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+<a id="CreditControl"><h1>Credit Control</h1></a>
+<p>
+Credit control applies the same rules to every customer every time, and keeps a history of what was decided, by whom and when. It covers credit reviews, putting customers on stop or on watch, overdue reminder letters, month end interest and late fees, and writing off bad debts. A customer's credit status is the credit status rating held on the customer record (see Credit Status Ratings) so the stop list is the same list the sales office, the web shop, electronic orders and standing orders all use.</p>
+<p>
+Credit control actions need the security token <i>Credit control</i> (21), given by default to the Accountant and System Administrator roles. Approving a write-off over the approval limit needs the token <i>Credit control approval</i> (22), given by default to the System Administrator role. Give these tokens to the roles used by the credit controller, the deputy and the Finance Director. Everyone else, including sales reps and the sales office, can see the credit information of the customers they are allowed to see but cannot change a credit limit or a credit status. A sales rep sees only the customers assigned to them.</p>
+
+<a id="CreditReviews"><h2>Credit reviews</h2></a>
+<p>
+From the customer selection screen choose <i>Credit Review and History</i>, or use <i>Credit Reviews and Stop/Watch</i> in the accounts receivable transactions menu. The page shows the customer's status, credit limit, balance, overdue amount, the last reminder stage sent and the next review date. Recording a review records the date, who did it, the outcome (limit confirmed, raised, reduced, put on watch, put on stop, taken off watch or stop), the new credit limit, notes and the next review date. If the review changes the limit the customer's limit is changed at the same time, so the limit and the reason for it are always recorded together. Reviews cannot be changed or deleted. The page also lists every review and everything else credit control has done for the customer.</p>
+<p>
+Every customer has a next review date. A new customer gets one automatically, one standard review period (set in the Credit Control Setup, normally 12 months) after the account is opened. When the system is first upgraded every existing customer is given a date one review period away. Customers with a date within the days chosen on the credit control report are listed as due.</p>
+
+<a id="StopWatch"><h2>Stop and watch</h2></a>
+<p>
+A customer on <b>stop</b> cannot be supplied by any route. No order can be entered in the sales office, placed through the web shop, received electronically or raised from a standing order, and goods cannot be dispatched or invoiced on orders taken before the stop, unless credit control releases the order from the credit control report. The reason is recorded when the customer is put on stop and when they are taken off. A customer on <b>watch</b> can still be supplied but everyone taking an order for them is warned, and credit control can see their orders on the credit control report. The customer is not told that they are on watch. The web shop shows a customer on stop that their account is on hold and that they should contact us.</p>
+<p>
+An order that takes a customer over their credit limit is accepted but held for credit control. It cannot be dispatched or invoiced until credit control releases it. This needs the system parameter <i>Check Credit Limits</i> to be set to warn or prohibit (it is not applied if it is turned off).</p>
+<p>
+The credit status ratings that put customers on watch or on stop are set up in Credit Status Maintenance. Each rating says what it does: supply as normal, put on watch, or put on stop.</p>
+<p>
+An invoice or charge can be marked as <i>disputed</i> on the credit review page. A disputed item is not chased and does not attract interest until the dispute is settled.</p>
+<p>
+Customers can be exempted from reminder letters, interest or late fees on the credit review page, with a reason.</p>
+
+<a id="DunningLetters"><h2>Overdue reminder letters</h2></a>
+<p>
+Finance sets the number of reminder stages, the days overdue that trigger each, and the wording of each letter, in the Credit Control Setup. The words {name}, {debtorno}, {company}, {currency}, {amount} and {invoices} in the wording are replaced for each customer. The standard stages are a polite reminder, a firmer second letter and a final notice.</p>
+<p>
+The reminder run is started by credit control from <i>Overdue Reminder Run</i>, normally weekly. It first shows who will get which letter. Untick the customers who should be left out this time, for example where a payment has been promised, then send. A customer moves up one stage at a time, and only while still overdue by the days set for the next stage. A customer who has paid the invoices they were reminded about starts again at stage 1. A customer is not sent a letter inside the least number of days between letters set in the setup. Disputed invoices are not chased. Letters go by email to the customer's contacts flagged to receive statements. Customers with no email address are listed with their letter text so that it can be printed and posted. Each letter sent is recorded against the customer, with the date, the stage, the invoices it covered and the amount, and the customer can see it in the customer portal. The setup can also put a customer on stop automatically when the final notice is sent.</p>
+
+<a id="InterestFees"><h2>Month end interest and late fees</h2></a>
+<p>
+Finance sets in the Credit Control Setup an annual interest rate, a late fee, and the number of days an invoice must be overdue before it is charged. The general ledger accounts for the interest and fee income are chosen in Company Preferences. At month end use <i>Month End Interest and Late Fees</i>. It shows what will be charged to whom. Interest is one twelfth of the annual rate on invoices overdue by more than the grace days (interest is not charged on interest or fees), and the fee is charged once per customer per month, converted to the customer's currency. Confirm the list to post the charges. Each charge is a customer transaction in the customer's own currency, shown on the account and the next statement as <i>Interest and Late Fee Charge</i>, and it is posted to the general ledger. A customer is never charged twice for the same month. A charge can be waived, with a reason. The waiver is allocated to the charge and reverses the general ledger entries.</p>
+
+<a id="WriteOffs"><h2>Bad debt write-offs</h2></a>
+<p>
+<i>Bad Debt Write-offs</i> writes off all or part of a customer's unpaid invoices and charges, with a reason. The write-off reduces the customer's balance (it is allocated to the oldest items first) and is posted to the bad debts account chosen in Company Preferences, with any exchange rate difference posted to the sales exchange variances account. A write-off above the approval limit set in the Credit Control Setup does nothing until the Finance Director approves it on the same page. The customer is put on stop automatically unless the box is unticked. A debt that is paid after it has been written off is entered as an ordinary receipt and shows on the customer account as an unallocated receipt. The <i>Bad Debt Write-offs Report</i> lists the write-offs over a period.</p>
+
+<a id="CreditControlReport"><h2>Credit control report</h2></a>
+<p>
+The credit control report lists the customers due or overdue for a credit review, the customers over their credit limit and by how much, the customers on watch or on stop, and the orders held for credit control, taken before a stop or taken for customers on watch. It can be limited to a sales rep and a currency and the totals are shown in the home currency. The Aged Debtors report also shows each customer's credit status, credit limit and last reminder stage.</p>
+
+<a id="CreditReconciliation"><h2>Month end reconciliation with the general ledger</h2></a>
+<p>
+The <i>Debtors Control Reconciliation</i> report compares the customer accounts with the debtors control account in the general ledger by transaction type, so that if they do not agree the type of posting that caused the difference can be seen. It also lists entries on the control account with no customer transaction behind them, such as manual journals, and customer transactions with no entry on the control account. Interest, fees, waivers and write-offs are customer transactions and post to the ledger by themselves so no manual journals are needed.</p>
+
+<a id="SalesStaffNotes"><h2>Notes for sales staff</h2></a>
+<p>
+A customer shown <b>On Watch</b> can still be supplied. When you take an order for them you will see a warning. Talk to the customer before they order if you can. A customer shown <b>On Stop</b> cannot be supplied. The order screen will not accept an order and goods cannot be dispatched or invoiced. Do not promise delivery. Contact credit control. If an order you enter takes a customer over their credit limit it is accepted but held until credit control releases it. You can see a customer's status, reason, limit, balance, overdue amounts and reminders for your own customers but you cannot change them.</p>
+<!-- Help End: CreditControl -->
